// A standalone `impl Trait for T { … }`'s method BODY is type-checked (body-coverage arc).
//
// It was not, for a long time, and nothing said so. `check_standalone_impl` validated the
// *contract* — arity and annotated types against the trait (E0015) — while the hoist that grafts
// these methods onto the target so they can be dispatched lives in the BACKENDS. Nothing on the
// checking path ever walked the statements in between, so a body could be arbitrarily wrong and
// still check clean, failing only at run time. The identical body written in an *in-body*
// `impl Trait { }` block WAS checked (those methods are flattened into the type's own `methods`),
// so the two spellings disagreed about whether your code was checked at all.
//
// This is the guard: the return type is `int`, the body answers a `string`.
// expect: error E0007 at 20:16
// expect: exit 1
trait Answer { fn answer(): int }
class Wrong {
    pub fn new(): Wrong { return Wrong {} }
}
impl Answer for Wrong {
    pub fn answer(): int {
        return "not an int"
    }
}
echo Wrong.new().answer()
